![The Red Baron](cover.webp)

As an exercise for running `cgi-bin` scripts I built this small
web framework called `Wren Baron`, alluding to the [Red Baron](https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Baron) anime.

As I demonstrated in the post about Golang, the `cgi-bin` directory lets you
run binaries compiled with other programming languages.

```ninjas
crear-un-cgi-en-golang | Crear un CGI en Golang | Un script de CGI en Golang | /blog/crear-un-cgi-en-golang/cover.png
```

However, what happens if the language has no cgi library?.

That is the case with _Wren_; being a niche language it has no
`cgi` libraries to compile a standalone executable.

So we have to use a bridge with `PHP`.

This bridge simply converts the environment variables to _JSON_
and passes them as parameters when running the script.

Also, using `2>&1` lets us capture the error messages
when running the script.

```php
<?php

session_start();

$env = escapeshellarg(json_encode([
    "get" => $_GET,
    "post" => $_POST,
    "server" => $_SERVER,
    "session" => session_id(),
    "pwd" => getcwd()
    ]));

$wren = "../wrenc index.wren $env 2>&1";

passthru($wren);
?>
```

In the wren script we take the parameter
and process the _JSON_; this way
anything printed to `stdout` is shown

```js
import "runtime" for Runtime
import "os" for Process
import "json" for JSON

var args = Process.allArguments
var env = JSON.parse(args[2])
var name = env["get"]["name"]

System.print("<h1>Hello From Wren %(Runtime.WREN_VERSION)</h1>")
System.print("<h2>This is rendered from <strong>Wren</strong></h2>")
System.print("<p>Hello <strong>%(name)</strong></p>")
```

The result is a plain HTML page

